        "summary": "Fixed the issue by modifying `src/SeleniumLibrary/__init__.py` to check if a plugin has an `event_firing_webdriver` attribute after loading it, and set it on the library instance.\n\n**Change made:**\n- In the `_parse_plugins` method of `SeleniumLibrary.__init__`, added a check after plugin creation: if the plugin has an `event_firing_webdriver` attribute, it's assigned to `self.event_firing_webdriver`.\n\n**How it works:**\nNow plugins can set `event_firing_webdriver` directly in their `__init__` method (inheriting from `LibraryComponent`), and the value will be automatically picked up by SeleniumLibrary. This matches the requested API:\n```python\nclass MyPlugin(LibraryComponent):\n    def __init__(self, ctx):\n        LibraryComponent.__init__(self, ctx)\n        self.event_firing_webdriver = WhatEverYouWant\n```\n\n**Tests:**\n- All 13 plugin tests pass, including the new `test_easier_event_firing_webdriver_from_plugin` test.",
        "turns": 12
      }
    }
